## Why This Is Asked

This question probes end-to-end design of a multi-tenant LLM-ops pipeline balancing latency, privacy, policy management, and observability. It assesses ability to design streaming prompts, per-tenant models, hot-updatable policies, drift alerts, and tests.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy engine
- Data locality and privacy controls
- Streaming prompt handling
- Dynamic model routing
- Drift detection and audits
- Tamper-evident logs and auditable trails

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: pick a model based on policy
function selectModel(tenant, policy) {
  const p = policy[tenant] || {};
  if (p.forceGuard) return 'safety-guarded';
  if (p.latencyBudget && p.latencyBudget < 75) return 'base';
  return p.preferredModel ?? 'base';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate drift triggers in production without constant retraining?
- What would be your approach to simulate hot policy updates during peak load?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical ability to design per-tenant budgets with latency guarantees, bursts, and data locality in a production-grade LLM gateway.

## Key Concepts

- Token bucket and backpressure
- Budget drift detection and alerting
- Regional data locality and routing
- Canary routing and model variant selection based on budget state

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal per-tenant token bucket (conceptual)
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, capacity) {
    this.rate = rate; this.capacity = capacity; this.tokens = capacity; this.last = Date.now();
  }
  refill() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const add = (now - this.last) * this.rate / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + add);
    this.last = now;
  }
  consume(n = 1) {
    this.refill();
    if (this.tokens >= n) { this.tokens -= n; return true; }
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate budgets against real traffic with drift scenarios?
- What observability would you add to detect bursts without SLA violations?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a safe, low-latency RAG with strict tenant isolation and data locality for Oracle/Instacart-scale deployments.

## Key Concepts
- Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) with per-tenant vector stores
- Data locality and isolation: region-bound embeddings, tenant-scoped caches
- Policy-driven source selection and TTL eviction; PII filters
- Latency budgeting and metrics: p95 targets, caching strategy
- Testing: synthetic workloads, drift checks, leakage tests

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example tenant store schema
type TenantStore = {
  tenantId: string;
  region: string;
  vectorStore: string; // per-tenant FAISS/Weaviate index
  cache: Map<string, CachedDoc>;
  policy: {
    allowedSources: string[];
    piiPolicy: 'strict'|'lenient';
    ttlMs: number;
  };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate no cross-tenant leakage in CI/CD?
- How would you design eviction to handle bursty traffic while preserving hot docs?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests practical decision-making for edge/cloud routing and privacy in a beginner context.

## Key Concepts

- Edge inference
- Simple policy
- PII detection
- Latency testing

## Code Example

```javascript
function routePrompt(p) {
  const isSmall = p.tokens <= 128;
  const hasPII = /PII_REGEX/.test(p.text);
  if (isSmall && !hasPII) return edgeInfer(p);
  return cloudInfer(p);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you adapt the policy for offline mode changes?
- What metrics would you log to monitor privacy and latency?

## Why This Is Asked

Designing a privacy-preserving telemetry layer for multi-tenant LLM systems is critical to prevent data leakage while enabling debugging, governance, and explainability. It tests the candidate's ability to balance observability with privacy, and to design a scalable, auditable data model.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant isolation in traces and metrics
- PII redaction and data-minimization in logs
- OpenTelemetry integration and span tagging
- Sampling, retention policies, and cost-aware observability
- Anomaly detection for abuse and SLA violations

## Code Example

```javascript
type TelemetryEvent = {
  tenantId: string;
  timestamp: string;
  eventType: string;
  inputHash: string;
  outputHash?: string;
  region?: string;
  latencyMs?: number;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this with synthetic tenants while ensuring no PII leaks?
- How would you audit for policy compliance across tenants and support incident response?
